{
  "dom_id": "step-q115880",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/bc5b29fca0521e3da39993d0359e857333611c68c35e49c037ca7af031288ba3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{\u2032}={y}^{2}(y+4)$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=(y+1)^{2}(y+4)$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=-y(y+1)(y+4)$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/cd73bc9f35e167537996a3b33b84471dcb8fefe2eccb4afd21ca55a23e6478fd.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}={y}^{2}(y+4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=(y+1)^{2}(y+4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{\u2032}=-y(y+1)(y+4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=-4,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=-1,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
